/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Raoult's law describes the solvent of a dilute solution well and the solute badly.
   - pi is the partial pressure of solute i in the gas (Pa); xi is its mole fraction in the liquid; Hi is Henry's constant on the mole-fraction scale (Pa or bar). fi is fugacity and ki is Henry's constant on the fugacity basis, in the same units
   - Hi is the limiting slope of the pi against xi curve as xi → 0.
   - The standard state behind Hi is a hypothetical pure liquid: the solute at xi = 1 behaving as it does at infinite dilution.
   - γi∞ is the activity coefficient of solute i at infinite dilution; Pisat is the vapour pressure of pure liquid i (Pa).
   - This ratio can be large.
   - In a binary solution the solvent is nearly pure and the solute is nearly absent.
   - p₁ is the partial pressure of component 1 (Pa); x₁ is its liquid mole fraction; P₁sat is its pure vapour pressure (Pa); H₁ is its Henry constant (Pa); A is a dimensionless Margules constant.
   - Check the two limits.
   - The Henry line is accurate only while the solute is dilute.
*/
