/* book exam traps: Reaching for Pisat for a gas held above its critical temperature, where no vapour pressure exists. Henry's constant is the correct reference there. | Treating H as dimensionless. On the mole-fraction scale it carries pressure units — Pa or bar — and must match the units of pi. | Using Hi and Pisat as if they were the same number. They coincide only when γi∞ = 1, which is rare for gases in water. | Applying pi = xi Hi far outside the dilute range. Above xi ≈ 0.05 the real curve bends away from the Henry line. | Getting the temperature sign wrong. For most gases in water H rises with T, so solubility falls on warming — the reason warm soda is flat. */
