/* book Worked example 2: Q: A gas condensate of methane(1), propane(2) and n-butane(3) with z = 0.30, 0.30, 0.40 enters a flash drum at 300 K and 20 bar. Critical data: methane Tc = 190.6 K, Pc = 45.99 bar, ω = 0.012; propane 369.8 K, 42.48 bar, 0.152; n-butane 425.1 K, 37.96 bar, 0.200. Use Wilson K-values to find the vapour fraction β and the phase compositions (the first pass of the loop).
   1. Wilson: Ki = (Pc,i/P) exp[5.373(1 + ωi)(1 − Tc,i/T)]. Methane: 2.2995 × exp(1.9829) = 16.70. Propane: 2.1240 × exp(−1.4401) = 0.503. n-Butane: 1.8980 × exp(−2.6886) = 0.129.
   2. Phase test: ΣziKi = 0.30 × 16.70 + 0.30 × 0.503 + 0.40 × 0.129 = 5.21 > 1, and Σzi/Ki = 0.018 + 0.596 + 3.100 = 3.71 > 1, so the feed splits into two phases.
   3. Rachford-Rice f(β) = Σzi(Ki − 1)/[1 + β(Ki − 1)]: f(0) = 4.213, f(0.30) = +0.178, f(0.40) = −0.074. Newton from β = 0.30 gives 0.3609, then 0.3679, then 0.3679 again.
   4. Liquid: xi = zi/[1 + β(Ki − 1)] gives x₁ = 0.30/6.776 = 0.0443, x₂ = 0.30/0.8172 = 0.3671, x₃ = 0.40/0.6796 = 0.5886; the sum is 1.0000.
   5. Vapour: yi = Kixi gives y₁ = 0.7393, y₂ = 0.1847, y₃ = 0.0759; the sum is 1.0000.
   6. The full phi-phi method now puts these x and y into the EOS, updates Ki = φiL/φiV, and repeats.
   Answer: With Wilson K-values (16.70, 0.503, 0.129) the vapour fraction is β = 0.368, the liquid is x = 0.044, 0.367, 0.589 and the vapour is y = 0.739, 0.185, 0.076: methane goes mostly to the vapour, n-butane to the liquid. */
