/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Put a pure solid 1 in a liquid and stir.
   - x₁ is the mole fraction of the solid in the saturated liquid; γ₁ is its activity coefficient in that liquid; Δhfus is the enthalpy of fusion of the pure solid in J/mol; R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; T is the temperature in K; Tm is the normal melt
   - Set γ₁ = 1 to get the ideal solubility.
   - Two liquid phases α and β form when unlike molecules attract each other much less than like molecules do, so GE is large and positive.
   - xiα and xiβ are mole fractions of component i in phases α and β; γiα and γiβ are the corresponding activity coefficients.
   - A tie line joins two liquid compositions that coexist.
   - Solvent extraction uses this.
   - When two liquid phases and a vapour are present, every component has equal fugacity in all three phases: fiL1 = fiL2 = fiV.
   - Two pure solids in a liquid give a eutectic.
   - Crystallisation uses cooling or evaporation to make x₁ exceed the solubility.
*/
