/* book Worked example 2: Q: Two solids A (Tm = 330 K, Δhfus = 20.0 kJ/mol) and B (Tm = 350 K, Δhfus = 25.0 kJ/mol) form an ideal liquid solution and completely immiscible solids. Find the eutectic temperature and composition.
   1. Each solid gives its own ideal solubility curve: xA = exp[−(20000/8.314)(1/T − 1/330)] and xB = exp[−(25000/8.314)(1/T − 1/350)].
   2. At the eutectic the liquid is saturated with both solids, so xA + xB = 1.
   3. Trial T = 310 K: xA = 0.6248 and xB = 0.3300, sum 0.9548, too low. Trial T = 312 K: xA = 0.6567, xB = 0.3512, sum 1.0079, too high.
   4. Interpolate: T = 310 + 2 × (1 − 0.9548)/(1.0079 − 0.9548) = 311.7 K. There xA = 0.652 and xB = 0.348, sum 1.000.
   Answer: The eutectic is at 311.7 K with liquid xA = 0.652 (xB = 0.348), below both melting points. */
